    {
      "id": "qtm-m3-variance-sd-c1",
      "heading": "What variance and standard deviation tell you",
      "explanation_md": "**Variance** is a measure of the **dispersion** of the possible values of the random variable **about the mean**. It gives an idea of the **variation or uncertainty** associated with the random variable.\n\n- Variance is a **squared quantity**, so it is often more useful to take its **square root**: the **standard deviation**.\n- When two random variables are compared, the one with the **larger variance (standard deviation)** is the **more variable** one.\n- **Investments:** of two investments with the **same expected return**, the one with the **higher standard deviation** is considered **riskier**. (A higher standard deviation means returns are more variable, both **below and above** the mean.)\n- In the calculation, the **probabilities of the values are used as weights** in computing the expected squared deviation from the mean.",
      "example_ids": [],
      "key_terms": [
        {
          "term": "Variance of a random variable",
          "meaning": "A measure of the dispersion of its possible values about the mean; the variation or uncertainty associated with it.",
          "source_ref": "qtm--module-3#40"
        },
        {
          "term": "Standard deviation of a random variable",
          "meaning": "The square root of the variance.",
          "source_ref": "qtm--module-3#40"
        }
      ],
      "exam_line": "Variance measures dispersion about the mean; standard deviation is its square root; with equal expected returns, the investment with the higher standard deviation is riskier.",
      "clarification": null,
